A||||19||Ken Wittlief||handling Q: does your car squirm?||||5||
Z||000000||Ken Wittlief||06-26-2001||10:54 AM||||I've noticed this from time to time since I got my '85 SE about, what? two months ago now.<P> sometimes when I pull out fast while turning it feels like my car - the only word I can think of for it is: squirms.<P> the car twists slightly in the turn, and twists back, almost as if the wheels are breaking loose, but not quite.<P>  It reminds me of when tires went from the old kind to radials. It took a while to get use to the new sideways squishy feel of radials, esp when turning sharply and suddenly.<P>  Is it just that Im not use to the short wheelbase of the fiero, so the radial side play is exaggerated? could my bushings be that loose?<P>  I also noticed if you move the wheel side to side at 40mph or faster, the car feels soft, like there is a few inches of give, then the sideways lateral traction tightens up.<P> I should point out I have the stock 14" tires, 70 profile I believe. So it could be mostly the radial tire play I am feeling, or it could be the soft pre '88 suppension. <P>  But it is kinda weird. When I really pull out fast on a turn I have to correct the steering slightly to compensate for the squirm.<P> Other than that the car corners like its glued to the pavement - reminds me of the old AFX slot cars, when they came out with the magnets that held the car to the track.<P>  Anyone else notice this?<P>EDIT: my tires are Michelin 195-70R14 all around on stock allow wheels.<p>[This message has been edited by Ken Wittlief (edited 06-26-2001).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||Leadfoot||06-26-2001||12:01 PM||||Not sure if your squirm is like mine but I have an 85 as well.  When I first got it it was like driving a water bead.  I did a suspension inspection and found out that all my rubber bushings were shot (especially the rear ones).  I replaced them all with Prothanes (~$150) and it handles nice and tight.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Slammed Fiero||06-26-2001||12:15 PM||||This is getting really technical , But it is a factor.<P>Tread pattern specifically the blocks of rubber can squirm depending on their design. This is common in smaller tires on heavier cars. The larger the blocks the more stable , the less squirm. While this is really getting down to specifics , it is a factor in the way your car handles.<P>JM||||reg||1
Z||000003||Will||06-26-2001||01:06 PM||||Our suburban has obsolete 16.5" wheels with antiquated tread design on the tires. The layout of the blocks allows them to flex in such a way that it causes a rather disconcerting transient oversteer.<P>Anyway, on topic, I'd suggest replacing cradle bushings with polyurethane or aluminum, then afterward replacing rear control arm bushings with poly, delrin, or UHMW.||||reg||1
Z||000004||84Bill||06-26-2001||01:14 PM||||I've noticed this too but I don't pay much attention to it anymore since my butt has been recalibrated to my 84. I noticed that if I hit a small bump on only one side of the car it kind of does a little squirmy dance. I attribute this to the bumpsteer inherrent in theese cars. It can be very uncomfortable to hit a bump or drive over uneven pavement in a turn. <p>[This message has been edited by 84Bill (edited 06-26-2001).]||||reg||14
Z||000005||Ken Wittlief||06-26-2001||01:27 PM||||oh yeah! ditto that! there is an on ramp in my town with a 1" pavement edge halfway through the turn. You catch that and the car skitters most haneiously.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Firefly||06-26-2001||03:03 PM||||my car feel squirmy since I put BF goodrich tires on, they hold better in rain, but the tire walls aren't as stiff.<BR>the Goodyear Eagles has a much tighter feel but doesn't hold well on wet roads.<P>you get the same squirmy feeling if tires are low on air.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Ken Wittlief||06-26-2001||03:18 PM||||The thing about it that makes me wonder whats really behind it is, its non-linear<P>it happens halfway through the turn, kinda all at once, as if something gave or slipped.<P>maybe the backwheels are breaking loose for an instant and regaining their grip - its hard to explain, you got your foot almost to the floor, making a tight turn, about halfway through, and the rear notches over about an inch or two, and then its holding tight again. you feel it more in the seat of your pants than anything else.<P>  weird!<p>[This message has been edited by Ken Wittlief (edited 06-26-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||V8Dreamer||06-26-2001||04:38 PM||||Your alignment might be off too, an 86 GT that my buddy bought a while ago had some suspension work done to it prior to him buying it and was never aligned.  Talk about squirrely feeling.<P>Ryan||||reg||1
Z||000009||Will||06-26-2001||04:47 PM||||My 86 SE with rubber cradle bushings and control arm bushings squirms, kicks sideways when powershifting, etc.<P>My '87 GT with aluminum cradle bushings and UHMW control arm bushings does not exhibit any of these bad behaviours.<P>Replace the cradle bushings with poly.||||reg||1
Z||000010||88formula||06-26-2001||05:11 PM||||My 87 coupe did the same thing. Of course its suspension was original at the time.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000011||84Bill||06-26-2001||05:36 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Firefly:<BR><B>my car feel squirmy since I put BF goodrich tires on, they hold better in rain, but the tire walls aren't as stiff.<BR>the Goodyear Eagles has a much tighter feel but doesn't hold well on wet roads.<P>you get the same squirmy feeling if tires are low on air.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I'll take the grippier tires anyday! I cant stand slick tires! <P>||||reg||1
Z||000012||Formula 18||06-27-2001||01:00 AM||||Oh Oh Oh... I got it!!!!!!!!<P>Drop throtle Oversteer, maybe?? <P>Do you let of the throtle in the middle of the corner? That's will cost a lot more than new rubber to fix. If you want to slove that problem get out your wallet. ||||reg||1
Z||000013||Ken Wittlief||06-27-2001||09:29 AM||||I dont thnk Im letting off the trottle when it happens - a few times I have let off after it happens: "what the H#ll was that?!"<P> Im not talking about going around curves at 80mph, Im talking about pulling out from a side street or making a left turn from a red light - from a stop to full throttle - Ive noticed it several times already.||||reg||1
Z||000014||lowCG||06-27-2001||10:46 PM||||Sounds like the rear control arm bushings,or possibly the front balljoints.<BR>Does the car,when the wheel is held tightly straight ahead,steer around when the throttle pressed on/off?||||reg||1
Z||000015||Sevaan||06-27-2001||11:06 PM||||I just put more air in the tires, and I haven't squirmed since.||||reg||1
Z||000016||Ken Wittlief||06-28-2001||01:43 PM||||lowCG, I tried it during lunch - hands tight on the wheel, or no hands on the wheel, both ways<P>  from no throttle to WFO and back again at 45mph the car tracks straight as an arrow - no pulling or anything<P> even when the auto kicks all the way down to second.||||reg||1
Z||000017||terryk||06-28-2001||03:34 PM||||I found that the 84-87 suspension seems to dislike loose single ply sidewalls. The car would randomly sway when driven in a straight line. Yok S306's.||||reg||1
Z||000018||lowCG||06-28-2001||10:28 PM||||So the balljoint thing is out after that test,never thought about the tires,but air pressure would give you an indication at least.When my car did this it was only the rear control arm bushings.<BR>Hard to really say for sure,but have you had the car aligned lately?||||reg||1
Z||000019||Ken Wittlief||06-29-2001||09:58 AM||||I dont know if the car has been aligned since it was new.<P> there are no 'other' indications of alignment being off - the tires are wearing normally, tracks straight as an arrow at 75mph...||||reg||1
